 "notes": "Libretto by Luigi Illica and Giuseppe Giacosa (1896). Words follow the Ricordi vocal score's spelling and punctuation (danaro; biricchin with its double c; Come!?; the print's runs of dots after E, signor, Cuor and biricchin). Musetta's \"date a me\" is printed with a trailing hyphen, as if cut off; it is given here as \"me\". The chorus (children, girls, students, townsfolk, sellers, the mothers and boys at the windows) sings in the score and is heard with the piano; only the soloists' lines are given here: the four friends, Musetta and Mimì. Alcindoro does not sing in this passage. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, words sung on one note joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ, intervocalic s voiced. Translation literal, for meaning.",
